# 1Password template — one file, all tiers.
#
# CI / prod: the environment segment is parameterized as $APP_ENV, so the SAME
# template resolves to CI or prod secrets by swapping one variable:
#
#   APP_ENV=<repo>-ci   op inject -i .env.op -o .env   # local / PR CI
#   APP_ENV=<repo>-prod op inject -i .env.op -o .env   # deploy
#
# In GitHub Actions, 1Password/load-secrets-action reads these refs with a
# service-account token scoped READ-ONLY to the matching Environment, so a
# leaked CI token can never read prod. See /sdlc:secrets for the setup.
#
# Dev workstation: use literal op://dev/<repo>/<field> refs (one shared `dev`
# vault — no parameterization). Resolved by `mise run setup` / `workon <repo>`
# via ~/.config/op/dev-token. See /sdlc:secrets §2 (Dev workstation tier).
#
# Copy to .env.op, fill in your op:// references, then inject.

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# EXAMPLE_TOKEN=op://$APP_ENV/example-item/token        # CI / prod
# EXAMPLE_TOKEN=op://dev/<repo>/example-token            # dev workstation

# ── Optional — each enables a feature ──────────────────────────────

# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=op://$APP_ENV/anthropic/key
# SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=op://$APP_ENV/slack/bot-token
